  "summary": "Buddhist icons painted to exact measure on cloth — mandalas, Buddhas and lineage trees for meditation.",
  "description": "A thangka is a painted or embroidered Buddhist banner mounted in silk brocade, rolled like a scroll and unrolled for teaching, initiation and visualization. Iconometry governs every Buddha's proportions; gridding precedes the laying of mineral pigments (lapis, malachite, cinnabar) in fine modeling with 24k gold outlining. Mandalas with palaces and deity cycles, Medicine Buddha fields, wheel-of-life diagrams and long lineage trees of tiny masters fill the repertoire. Monastery workshops from Lhasa to Kathmandu and Rebgong keep lineage styles distinct and living.",
  "culturalContext": "Tibetan Buddhist painting developed from widespread traditions of early Buddhist paintings which now only survive in a few sites such as the Ajanta Caves in India and the Mogao Caves in China, which have very extensive wall-paintings and were the repository for what are now the earliest surviving Tibetan paintings on cloth. The thanka form developed alongside the tradition of Tibetan Buddhist wall paintings, which are or were mostly in monasteries.",
